  "text": "# External Review\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Conditionally worth doing — but the current design would not test your thesis.**\n\nThe essay idea is publishable and the framing is attractive. The experiment as described is not yet an experiment; it is an observation session. Two structural problems dominate:\n\n1. **No contrast arm.** Your thesis is comparative (\"agents inherit the game\"). A single benign-scenario run cannot support it, no matter how carefully observed. You need at least the profit-framed condition you are implicitly criticizing, run by you, on your own environment.\n2. **No temptation, no ground truth.** If the beneficial scenario contains no opportunity to defect, hide information, or game a proxy, then observing good behavior tells you nothing. And if the outcome is prose rather than a simulated quantity, \"solution quality\" collapses into taste.\n\nBoth are fixable, and fixing them turns a soft essay into a small but genuinely citable result. There is also a framing risk worth naming early: the claim that the field's \"common conclusion\" from vending-machine-style evals is *agents are unsafe/evil* is close to a strawman. Several of those results are primarily about **long-horizon coherence, memory decay, and reliability**, not moral character. If you open by mischaracterizing them, technically literate readers will stop at paragraph three.\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\nScale note: for *Risk of overclaiming*, 10 = well guarded against overclaiming, 1 = actively invites it.\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raw novelty | **4/10** | \"Agents reproduce the incentive structure you place them in\" is close to consensus and has prior instrumentation (reward-vs-ethics benchmarks, Goodhart literature, emergent-collusion work); the fresh part is not the thesis but the paired prose-vs-contract governance manipulation. |\n| Concrete evidence potential | **6/10** | High *if* you build a deterministic simulator with a hidden welfare function and planted temptations; low (≈3) if agents merely produce recommendations that you read and grade. |\n| Technical precision | **3/10** | \"Same broad objective,\" \"may share if they choose,\" \"improve the final solution,\" and \"drift into rhetoric\" are currently unoperationalized — none of them yet map to a number, a log line, or a decision rule. |\n| Practitioner usefulness | **6/10** | Builders would genuinely value a measured \"governance tax\" (quality and token cost of contracts vs violations prevented); they would not value another qualitative transcript walkthrough. |\n| Narrative strength | **7/10** | \"The vending machine wasn't the problem; the scoreboard was\" is a strong, legible hook — docked for the strawman risk and for the mo"
